I had this issue too when using a local modal, in my case I was able to solve it by specifing the input limit. 
```json
"models": {
  "Qwen3.6-27B-Fast": {
    "name": "Qwen3.6 27B Fast (MTP 40K)",
    "limit": {
      "context": 40000,
      "input": 40000,
      "output": 32000
    }
  },
```
Could be related to https://github.com/anomalyco/opencode/issues/13980

> > Blocks execution of those tools but does not remove them from the tool list sent to the model. The model still sees all tool definitions and they still count toward the limit.
> 
> This is not true, the permissions will omit them from context

I actually dug into the opencode source to verify this, and the claim that "permissions omit them from context" doesn't match the current implementation.
The smoking gun is in `packages/opencode/src/session/llm.ts`:

```typescript
const ruleset = Permission.merge(input.agent.permission ?? [], input.permission ?? [])
workflowModel.sessionPreapprovedTools = Object.keys(prepared.tools).filter((name) => {
  const match = ruleset.findLast((rule) => Wildcard.match(name, rule.permission))
  return !match || match.action !== "ask"
})
```
What it actually does: `prepared.tools` contains every MCP and built-in tool before any filtering. The code only strips out "ask" tools — both "allow" and "deny" pass through unchanged. That full record then gets mapped in `native-runtime.ts` and sent directly to the model. There's a UI filter in `registry.ts`, but it only hides denied tools from the TUI sidebar; it doesn't touch the LLM payload.
Right now the split is clear: the LLM receives all the definitions (and they count against the tool limit), but the runtime blocks the actual calls.

@rekram1-node can you show me the exact file and lines where this logic is implemented?
